<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"

         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnRisky="true"
         failOnNotice="true"
><!--
        Advisory checks are forbidden: a gate that only warns is ignored within
        a fortnight. PHPUnit's default is exactly that — it reports issues and
        exits 0 — so these three turn its own findings into build failures.

        WHAT THESE DO NOT COVER, measured rather than assumed. A CI run printed

            Tests:  19 warnings (38 assertions)

        and the pipeline called the gate passed. Adding the attributes below did
        NOT change that exit code, because PHPUnit never saw an issue: run
        directly, `vendor/bin/phpunit` reports `OK` on the very same code. The
        warnings are surfaced by Collision, which `php artisan test` runs
        through and which reports PHP diagnostics that PHPUnit's handler
        discards — including ones raised under `@`.

        So the guard against a warning-flavoured green lives in
        scripts/quality.sh, which inspects the summary line. These attributes
        are still correct, and still worth having for issues PHPUnit does own —
        they are simply not what closes that hole.

        `failOnDeprecation` is deliberately NOT set: a deprecation raised inside
        a dependency would fail this build for something no one here can fix in
        the same commit, which turns a red build into noise to be routed around.
-->

    <!--
        RS-040 defines four homes for tests. Three of them are PHP and belong
        here; the fourth is frontend and runs under its own tooling.

        `Integration` holds contract tests that cross a module boundary — the
        tests that prove the module-to-module contracts hold, which is the
        single most important thing to verify in a modular monolith. If nothing
        declares that directory, PHPUnit never collects it: such a test would
        be written, committed, reported as passing by a green suite, and never
        once executed.

        Each directory carries a tracked .gitkeep. Git does not track empty
        directories, and PHPUnit aborts the entire run — not skips — when a
        declared testsuite directory is absent:

            Test directory "/var/www/html/tests/Unit" not found

        That failed a CI run: tests/Unit existed on the development machine as
        leftover scaffolding and was invisible to git, so the suite ran locally
        and could not run from a clean clone.
    -->
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory>tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Integration">
            <directory>tests/Integration</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS" value="4"/>
        <env name="BROADCAST_CONNECTION" value="null"/>
        <!--
            These two lines do NOT apply inside the isolated test stack.

            PHPUnit's `<env>` never overwrites a variable already present in
            the environment (without `force="true"`), and
            docker/compose/compose.test.yaml exports `CACHE_STORE: redis` to
            the runner — so Redis wins, not `array`. That is intended and
            stays: the cache is tested on the engine that ships (the same
            reasoning as MySQL over SQLite below).

            Its effect on isolation is handled by `Tests\TestCase::setUp`,
            which flushes the cache before every test — because
            `RefreshDatabase` rolls back the database and knows nothing about
            the cache. The two lines remain for a run outside the stack, where
            no environment variable precedes them.
        -->
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="array"/>
        <env name="CACHE_LIMITER" value="array"/>

        <!--
            Tests run on MySQL, not on in-memory SQLite.

            Laravel's skeleton default is sqlite::memory: and it is faster, but
            it voids what is being tested: an append-only guarantee enforced by
            MySQL triggers does not exist on SQLite — the tests pass green while
            the constraint is absent.

            Testing on an engine other than the production engine is false
            confidence. The *_test database is created by docker/mysql/initdb
            for exactly this purpose, and the isolated stack is torn down after
            the run.
        -->
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="mysql"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="app_test"/>
        <env name="DB_URL" value=""/>
        <env name="MAIL_MAILER" value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>
        <env name="PULSE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="TELESCOPE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="NIGHTWATCH_ENABLED" value="false"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
